ire Aubame","from_t":1964.1311475409836,"to_t":1964.1338797814208,"title":"President of Gabon","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Jean-Hilaire Aubame"},{"qid":"Q1000","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q2662541","value_label":"Didjob Divungi Di Ndinge","from_t":2009.4328767123288,"to_t":2009.4383561643835,"title":"President of Gabon","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Didjob Divungi Di Ndinge"},{"qid":"Q1000","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q270645","value_label":"Rose Francine Rogombe","from_t":2009.4383561643835,"to_t":2009.7890410958903,"title":"President of Gabon","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Rose Francine Rogombe"},{"qid":"Q1000","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q299629","value_label":"Léon M'ba","from_t":1960.6256830601094,"to_t":1964.1284153005465,"title":"President of Gabon","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Léon M'ba"},{"qid":"Q1000","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q299629","value_label":"Léon M'ba","from_t":1964.1338797814208,"to_t":1967.9041095890411,"title":"President of Gabon","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Léon M'ba"},{"qid":"Q1000","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q57521","value_label":"Ali Bongo Ondimba","from_t":2009.7890410958903,"to_t":2023.6602739726027,"title":"President of Gabon","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Ali Bongo Ondimba"},{"qid":"Q1000","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q101769","value_label":"Flag of Gabon.svg","from_t":1960.603825136612,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Gabon","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Gabon"}],"people":{"Q122067521":{"label":"Brice Clotaire Oligui Nguema","description":"President of Gabon since 2023","enwiki":"Brice Oligui Nguema","summary":"Brice Clotaire Oligui Nguema (French pronunciation: [bʁis klɔ.tɛʁ ɔ.li.gi ŋgɛ.ma]; born 3 March 1975) is a Gabonese politician and military officer who is currently serving as the fourth president of Gabon since May 2025, having previously served in this role in a transitional capacity from 2023 and was also the chairman of the Committee for the Transition and Restoration of Institutions from 2023 until his accession to the presidency. He has also been the commander-in-chief of the Gabonese Republican Guard since 2020.\nOligui is a member of the Bongo family and played a key part in overthrowing his cousin Ali Bongo during the 2023 coup. He ran for president in Gabon's first democratic election in five decades and was elected.","image":"Brice Oligui Nguema on November 26, 2024 (cropped).jpg","birth":1975.1671232876713,"death":null},"Q57856":{"label":"Raymond Ndong Sima","description":"Gabonese politician","enwiki":"Raymond Ndong Sima","summary":"Raymond Ndong Sima (born 23 January 1955) is a Gabonese politician who served as transitional Prime Minister of Gabon from September 2023 to May 2025. He was previously prime minister from February 2012 to January 2014.","image":"Raymond Ndong Sima 2024.jpg","birth":1955.0602739726028,"death":null},"Q172083":{"label":"Omar Bongo","description":"Gabonese politician (1935–2009)","enwiki":"Omar Bongo","summary":"Omar Bongo Ondimba (born Albert-Bernard Bongo; 30 December 1935 – 8 June 2009) was the second president of Gabon, serving from 1967 until his death in 2009. A member of the Gabonese Democratic Party (PDG), Bongo was promoted to key positions as a young official under Gabon's first president Léon M'ba in the 1960s, before being made the second vice president in 1966, replacing Paul-Marie Yembit. In 1967, after M'ba's death, he became the country's president.\nBongo headed the single-party regime of the PDG until 1990, when, faced with public pressure, he was forced to introduce multi-party politics into Gabon.","image":"Omar Bongo, the President of Gabon in Brussels on February 20, 1993 (1, cropped).jpg","birth":1935.9945205479453,"death":2009.4328767123288},"Q256998":{"label":"Jean-Hilaire Aubame","description":"Gabonese politician (1912-1989)","enwiki":"Jean-Hilaire Aubame","summary":"Jean-Hilaire Aubame (10 November 1912 – 18 August 1989) was a Gabonese politician active during both the colonial and independence periods. The French journalist Pierre Péan said that Aubame's training \"as a practicing Catholic and a customs official helped to make him an integrated man, one of whom political power was not an end in itself.\"\nBorn into a Fang family, Aubame was orphaned at a young age. He was raised by the stepbrother of Léon M'ba, who became Aubame's chief political rival. Encouraged by his colleagues, Aubame entered politics, serving as Gabon's first representative in the National Assembly of France from 1946 to 1958.","image":"Aubame.jpg","birth":1912.8579234972678,"death":1989.6219178082192},"Q2662541":{"label":"Didjob Divungi Di Ndinge","description":"Gabonese politician","enwiki":"Didjob Divungi Di Ndinge","summary":"Didjob Divungi Di Ndinge (born 5 May 1946) is a Gabonese politician who was Vice-President of Gabon from 1997 to 2009. He is the President of the Democratic and Republican Alliance (ADERE), a political party. As Vice-President of Gabon, Divungi Di Ndinge exercised presidential powers in an acting capacity from May 2009 to June 2009, while President Omar Bongo Ondimba was hospitalized.","image":null,"birth":1946.3397260273973,"death":null},"Q270645":{"label":"Rose Francine Rogombe","description":"Gabonese politician (1942-2015)","enwiki":"Rose Francine Rogombé","summary":"Rose Francine Rogombé (née Etomba; 20 September 1942 – 10 April 2015) was a Gabonese politician who was acting president of Gabon from June to October 2009, following the death of long-time President Omar Bongo. She constitutionally succeeded Bongo due to her role as president of the Senate, a post to which she was elected in February 2009. She was a lawyer by profession and a member of the Gabonese Democratic Party (PDG). Rogombé was the first female head of state of Gabon.","image":null,"birth":1942.717808219178,"death":2015.2712328767122},"Q299629":{"label":"Léon M'ba","description":"Prime Minister (1959–61) and President (1961–67) of Gabon","enwiki":"Léon M'ba","summary":"Gabriel Léon M'ba (9 February 1902 – 28 November 1967) was the first president of Gabon, serving from 1961 until his death in 1967. He also served as the first prime minister of Gabon from 1959 to 1961.\nA member of the Fang ethnic group, M'ba was born into a relatively privileged village family. After studying at a seminary, he held a number of small jobs before entering the colonial administration as a customs agent.","image":"Leon M'ba, 1962. D789-083 (cropped).jpg","birth":1902.1068493150685,"death":1967.9068493150685},"Q57521":{"label":"Ali Bongo Ondimba","description":"President of Gabon (2009–2023)","enwiki":"Ali Bongo","summary":"Ali Bongo Ondimba (born Alain-Bernard Bongo; 9 February 1959) is a Gabonese former politician and dictator who was the third president of Gabon from 2009 until he was deposed in a coup in 2023. A member of the Gabonese Democratic Party, Bongo is the son of Omar Bongo, who was president from 1967 until his death in 2009.\nDuring his father's presidency, Bongo was Minister of Foreign Affairs from 1989 to 1991, represented Bongoville as a deputy in the National Assembly from 1991 to 1999, and was the Minister of National Defense from 1999 to 2009. After his father's death, Bongo was elected president in the 2009 presidential election, marking the first political dynasty in the country.","image":"Ali Bongo on December 14, 2022 (cropped).jpg","birth":1959.1068493150685,"death":null}},"media":{"Flag of Gabon.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Flag_of_Gabon.svg/330px-Flag_of_Gabon.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Gabon.svg","author":"Léon Augé and Pierre Claver Eyeghe","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Gabon.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Coat_of_arms_of_Gabon.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Gabon.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Gabon.svg","author":"Original by Louis Mühlemann, vectorised by user:Fenn-O-maniC","license":"Public domain"},"Brice Oligui Nguema on November 26, 2024 (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Brice_Oligui_Nguema_on_November_26%2C_2024_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Brice_Oligui_Nguema_on_November_26%2C_2024_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brice_Oligui_Nguema_on_November_26,_2024_(cropped).jpg","author":"Lukasz Kobus / European Communities, 2024 / EC - Audiovisual Service","license":"CC BY 4.0"},"Raymond Ndong Sima 2024.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Raymond_Ndong_Sima_2024.jpg/330px-Raymond_Ndong_Sima_2024.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raymond_Ndong_Sima_2024.jpg","author":"Gabon 24","license":"CC BY 3.0"},"Omar Bongo, the President of Gabon in Brussels on February 20, 1993 (1, cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Omar_Bongo%2C_the_President_of_Gabon_in_Brussels_on_February_20%2C_1993_%281%2C_cropped%29.jpg/330px-Omar_Bongo%2C_the_President_of_Gabon_in_Brussels_on_February_20%2C_1993_%281%2C_cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omar_Bongo,_the_President_of_Gabon_in_Brussels_on_February_20,_1993_(1,_cropped).jpg","author":"© European Union 2015 - Source : EP","license":"European Parliament"},"Aubame.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Aubame.jpg/330px-Aubame.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_cont